Build a debug terminal command that does not name /usr/bin/env.

prepareCommand assembles the shell line the debug adapter asks the terminal to
run. For a POSIX shell it sets the child's environment by prefixing the command
with /usr/bin/env, which is right everywhere it was written for and wrong here:
Android has no /usr/bin at all. A command naming an absolute path that does not
exist is not a lookup failure the shell can recover from, so bash answers

  bash: /usr/bin/env: No such file or directory

and the program never starts. Measured on an API 37 emulator with the three
launch configurations this app seeds: the session starts, the debug toolbar
appears, the status bar names the configuration, and the only sign that nothing
ran is one line in the terminal panel. That is worse than an error, because
every visible indicator says it is working.

Inline assignments and unset do the same job with no binary. `A=1 B=2 cmd` is
POSIX and is what a shell is for; the removals become `unset A B ; ` ahead of
the command. The one behavioural difference is that unset affects the shell
that runs the command rather than only the child, which matters only if the
terminal is reused for something else afterwards, and a debug terminal is not.

A name a shell cannot assign to is skipped rather than emitted, which is the
one place this cannot match what env does. env takes NAME=VALUE as a single
argument and calls execve itself, so any name without an '=' reaches the child;
a shell treats a word as an assignment only when the part before '=' is a valid
name, and runs it as a command otherwise. A project whose .env holds `my-var=1`
would therefore stop the program starting, with the debug toolbar up and the
session hanging, which is this patch's own symptom arriving by another door, and
a name holding a semicolon would run what follows it. Dropping the variable is
the mild outcome and the only one available without env.

Only the VALUE is quoted, and that is the whole of the difference between this
and the first version of it. `env` takes NAME=VALUE as a single argument, so
quoting the pair is correct there; a shell reads a quoted pair as one word and
tries to execute it, which on the device read as

  bash: NODE_OPTIONS= --require .../bootloader.js: No such file or directory

one layer further along and looking like a different bug. The key is left
unquoted because `'NAME'=value` is not an assignment to a shell, and the names
reaching `unset` need no quoting either: the guard above has already reduced
them to letters, digits and underscores.

